{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments ["<b>And she conceived.</b> There is no doubt, as we have mentioned, that Aaron was older than Moses in years, and thus our ancestors received that Puah was Miriam, and it is written \"and his sister stood afar off\" (below, verse 4). Now we know that the beginning of the count of four hundred years was from the day Isaac was born, for he alone is called seed, and at sixty years old he begot Jacob (Genesis 25:26), and Jacob begot Reuben at eighty-five, and he was one hundred and thirty years old when he went down to Egypt (ibid. 47:9). We add upon them the sixty of Isaac, totaling 190, and there remain 210. We subtract from them the seventy years that Joseph lived thereafter, and there remain 140. And Levi's years at his going down to Egypt were 43, and he lived 22 years after Joseph, and there remain 117, for it is written, and Joseph died at one hundred and ten years (ibid. 50:26). And after [Joseph] died, a new king arose (Exodus 1:8), and how many years they built treasure cities, and near the birth of Moses the decree was decreed upon the males. And Ben Zuta said: whoever recounts a matter of deed does not show favoritism to the great to mention him first. And it is written: \"she bore to Amram Aaron and Moses and Miriam their sister\" (Numbers 26:59) — and she is the youngest. Now this one stricken with blindness forgot the names of Ham and Japheth, for it is written \"and Noah begot\" (Genesis 5:32), and Japheth is the eldest. And further: \"there they buried Abraham and Sarah his wife\" (ibid. 49:31) — and she was buried in the cave thirty-eight years before Abraham. Now know that the letter nun will be found defective, or assimilated with a dagesh at the beginning of a word, like the nun of \"גש הלאה\" (Genesis 19:9), \"ויגש אליו\" (ibid. 44:18), and in the middle, as in \"וגם אף עלה בישראל\" (Psalms 78:21) or \"ויחר אף\" (ibid. 106:40) — and the nun is assimilated in the middle, therefore the pei receives a dagesh: \"ויחר אפו\" (Genesis 39:19), \"ישוב אַפְךָ\" (Isaiah 12:1). And so the nun of נתן, in the word תִּתִּי (Genesis 29:19), for it is like תנתי, for the second tav is not a root letter, but is like שבתי (Psalms 139:2), לכתי (1 Kings 2:5), לדתי. Also at the end: תת פחדך (Deuteronomy 2:25), טוב תתי אותה לך (Genesis 29:19). And likewise with nouns, for בת (daughter) should properly be a feminine form like from בֵן — בָנָה, and it would intermix with \"אשר בנה בית\" (Deuteronomy 25[?]). They exchanged the heh for a tav, like גבירה / גברת, עטרה / עטרת — once in construct state and once not in construct state, like עטרת תפארת (Proverbs 4:9). They dropped the nun to lighten the pronunciation and said בת. And the word רחל בתו (Genesis 29:6) is like אמת חסדו and אמתו — and the tav is feminine, and the nun is assimilated. The plural is אמונות, not אמתות and not אמיתיות, like בנות, and not בתות and not בתים:" "<b>And she conceived.</b> Scripture did not mention the birth of Miriam and Aaron, for nothing new happened at their birth. And the word טוב, as applied to a man full of understanding, can refer to the soul, like \"טוב עם יהוה\" and good with men, or טוב עין (Proverbs 22:9), or in the body, or in appearance — טוב תואר or מראה. But since we find concerning the child that he was good, it cannot be interpreted except as referring to his form: good beyond all other newborns:" "<b>Three months.</b> Some say that in the seventh month from the beginning of pregnancy he was born; this too is a midrash, for the Egyptians could not know when the woman conceived. In my opinion, Scripture recounts the months that she was able to hide him, for there is no power in man to see in a woman when she will give birth. For it is far-fetched that he was born in the seventh month from the beginning of pregnancy, for those born thus are short of stature, and those born in the eleventh month are more viable and robust, for most births are near nine months. And he who knows the time of conception can know the time of birth, and he who knows the time of birth can know the time of conception, for this is a matter tested by the ancients, and five times I myself have tested it: for the position of the moon and its elevation at the moment of conception — that is the elevation of the planetary position at the moment of birth; and the elevation [that obtains] at the moment of conception, there the moon will be at the moment of the molad. The small cycle is 29 days and one-third of a day, the middle one is 253, and the large one, which is the longest, is 287, and the scholars of nativities will agree. There is no objection from the tribes, for the fact that they are listed in order is no proof, for there are many such cases in the Torah. Thus it is possible that Rachel gave her handmaid to Jacob when she bore Simeon, and Issachar was also born before Asher, and Dinah was born in one womb with Zebulun. Therefore the word \"ואחר ילדה בת\" (Genesis 30:21) is like \"ואחרי כן יצא אחיו\" (ibid. 25[:25-26]), where Scripture did not mention ותהר for each one. Likewise לתקפות הימים (1 Samuel 1:20): [the birth was] after three [months]."], :book :exodus, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 2, :notes {0 "Ibn Ezra quotes Deuteronomy 25 for אשר בנה בית; the phrase אשר בנה בית appears at Deuteronomy 20:5 and 22:8, not 25. Citation left as in the source.", 2 "The closing phrase לתקפות הימים שלש הי׳ is elliptical; rendered hyper-literally. Ibn Ezra appears to draw an analogy to Hannah's pregnancy, but the argument is compressed and the text may be truncated."}, :source-witnesses [["Piotrkow, 1907-1911" "https://www.nli.org.il/he/books/NNL_ALEPH990020973480205171/NLI"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T18:31:18.488235084Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}